Theory of Computation
Course Syllabus and 15-Week Schedule
Course Description
This course introduces the formal models used to define what a computation is and what its limits are. It covers three main areas in sequence: automata and formal languages, computability, and complexity theory. Each unit builds on the previous one — finite automata motivate the need for more powerful models, which in turn motivates the question of what no model can do, and finally what can be done efficiently.
Primary Text
Michael Sipser, Introduction to the Theory of Computation, 3rd edition, Cengage Learning. Chapter and section numbers below refer to this edition. An older edition can be used, but numbering of examples may differ slightly.
Learning Outcomes
•	Construct and reason about finite automata, regular expressions, and context-free grammars.
•	Prove that a language is not regular or not context-free using the pumping lemma.
•	Explain the Turing machine model and the Church–Turing thesis, and trace TM computations.
•	Prove that a problem is undecidable using diagonalization or reduction.
•	Classify problems by time complexity and prove NP-completeness using reductions from known NP-complete problems.
Assessment Breakdown
•	Problem sets (4, biweekly): 30%
•	Quizzes (2, short): 10%
•	Midterm examination: 20%
•	Term project or presentation: 10%
•	Final examination: 30%
