Notes on Delivery
•	Weeks 1–8 (automata and formal languages) carry the heaviest proof-writing load for students meeting formal proofs for the first time. Budget extra worked examples here rather than in later weeks.
•	The pumping lemma (weeks 5 and 8) is consistently the point where students disengage if the proof structure is not made explicit step by step. Present it as a fixed template: assume regularity, pick a string, apply the pumping condition, derive a contradiction.
•	Diagonalization (week 12) benefits from a direct link back to the halting problem proof rather than introducing it as an abstract technique first.
•	The term project (announced by week 9) can be a short paper reducing one problem to another not covered in lecture, or an implementation of a Turing machine simulator.
Prerequisites
Discrete mathematics covering sets, relations, functions, induction, and basic proof techniques. Familiarity with asymptotic notation is useful for the complexity unit but can be reviewed in week 13 if needed.
